/** \page asyncrecompute Async recompute threading rules

Async recompute allows some document recomputes to run on a worker thread.
That changes the threading contract for code that can run during recompute or
observe recompute-side state.

\section asyncrecompute_scope Scope

The current async recompute implementation is request-based. A recompute
request may run on a worker thread when the request target is explicitly marked
as worker-safe. Worker-safety is determined through
App::DocumentObject::canRecomputeOnWorker().

Objects that are not known to be worker-safe are expected to return false. In
that case the recompute request falls back to the main thread instead of being
queued to the worker.

\section asyncrecompute_cpp Rules for C++ code

- Code that runs from DocumentObject::execute(), document recompute hooks, or
  any other recompute-side callback must be safe to execute concurrently with
  the GUI thread and with other unrelated asynchronous activity.
- Code that touches Qt widgets, view providers, FreeCADGui APIs, event loops,
  timers, network callbacks, or any other thread-affine state must stay on the
  main thread.
- If an object's recompute path is not worker-safe, override
  App::DocumentObject::canRecomputeOnWorker() to return false.
- Document-scoped signals in App::Document use App::MainThreadSignal and are
  intended for observers that need main-thread delivery, especially GUI code.
- Raw App::DocumentObject signals remain plain fastsignals with same-thread
  semantics. GUI code should not subscribe to them directly.

\section asyncrecompute_signal_policy Signal delivery policy

App::MainThreadSignal takes a per-signal App::DeliveryPolicy so the decision to
block the emitting thread is explicit and reviewed at the declaration:

- `Blocking` (default): hop to the main thread and wait. Required for non-void
  signals and for observers that must run before the emitter continues. A
  worker that emits a Blocking signal parks on the GUI thread, so a GUI-side
  wait on the worker must abandon the parked hop instead of deadlocking (see
  the note below).
- `Queued`: hop without waiting. Document/DocumentObject/Property references
  are captured as stable identity handles and resolved back to live objects on
  the main thread; a destroyed target is dropped instead of dereferenced.
- `Coalesced`: like Queued, but repeated emissions that resolve to the same
  identity collapse into a single delivery. Use only when observers need the
  latest state, not every transition.

The policy applied to each document signal:

| Signal | Policy | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| signalBeforeChange, signalChanged | Blocking | main-thread document properties |
| signalNewObject, signalActivatedObject, signalTransactionAppend | Blocking | add/activate object on the main thread |
| signalDeletedObject, signalTransactionRemove | Blocking | observers must run before the object is destroyed |
| signalBeforeChangeObject | Queued | only the aggregated App signal observes it |
| signalChangedObject | Coalesced | per-property, high frequency during recompute |
| signalTouchedObject | Coalesced | hottest signal (fine-grained dependency propagation) |
| signalRelabelObject | Queued | low frequency |
| signalRecomputedObject | Coalesced | one delivery per object per recompute burst |
| signalBeforeRecompute, signalRecomputed, signalBecameStable, signalSkipRecompute | Blocking | recompute barriers; also flush earlier queued deliveries |
| signalChangePropertyEditor | Queued | property-editor refresh |
| signalLinkXsetValue | Queued | external-link value update |
| save/restore/import/export signals | Blocking | file I/O on the main thread |

Because a blocking delivery is posted to the same GUI event queue as queued
deliveries, the trailing `signalRecomputed`/`signalBecameStable` barriers are
processed after the queued per-object notifications they follow.

\section asyncrecompute_pending_removals Pending removals

Objects removed while a recompute is in progress are queued in
DocumentP::pendingRemove and removed afterwards by
Document::processPendingRemovals(). That sweep mutates the document and emits
`signalDeletedObject` / `signalTransactionRemove`, whose observers must run
before the objects are destroyed, so it must run on the main thread. When
recompute runs on the worker, the worker hands the sweep to the main thread
with a blocking hop and waits; because the worker is parked, no other recompute
runs concurrently, which serializes the sweep against recompute. This replaces
the previous per-object deletion hops from the worker.

\note Residual blocking hops. `signalBeforeRecompute` and the
`signalRecomputed` / `signalBecameStable` barriers, plus the one blocking
hand-off of `processPendingRemovals`, remain Blocking on the worker. A worker
parked in one of these would otherwise hold up
Application::cancelRecomputeRequestsForDocument(), which blocks the main thread
in closeDocument() and cannot service the hop. That deadlock is closed by the
GUI invoker: while the main thread is waiting for the worker it sets
MainThreadSignalConfig::setMainThreadWaiting(true), and a blocking delivery
whose emitting thread is parked at that point is abandoned rather than run. The
worker then continues and the posted event becomes a no-op if it is ever
dispatched. The same mechanism covers Application destruction, which joins the
worker.

\section asyncrecompute_subject Observer lists

Base::Subject snapshots its observer set under a mutex and calls OnChange()
with the lock released, matching Base::ConsoleSingleton. This matters for
Base::ParameterGrp: a Python-backed object can write a parameter from a worker
recompute, which calls ParameterGrp::Notify() while GUI widgets attach/detach
observers on the main thread.

\section asyncrecompute_python Rules for Python-backed objects

Python-backed features may execute from the recompute worker. The GIL is kept
for the duration of document recompute so Python execution stays serialized, but
that does not make GUI access worker-safe.

- App::FeaturePython objects default to worker-unsafe and therefore stay on the
  main thread unless they explicitly opt in.
- Python execute() implementations must not access Qt UI or FreeCADGui objects
  from a worker thread.
- Worker-thread GUI access should fail fast through the guarded FreeCADGui and
  ViewObject entry points instead of proceeding silently.

\section asyncrecompute_review Review checklist

When changing recompute-related code, check at least the following:

- Does this code run during recompute, or observe state that recompute mutates?
- Does it touch GUI, Qt event-loop state, or any other thread-affine API?
- Should this object override canRecomputeOnWorker()?
- Is GUI-facing code subscribed to document-scoped main-thread signals instead
  of raw DocumentObject signals?
- Does the change introduce a new background callback path such as QTimer,
  QThread, QNetwork, QtConcurrent, or a custom worker that can now overlap with
  recompute?

*/
